.Sh DESCRIPTION
The KEYMGMT operation doesn't have much public visibility in OpenSSL
libraries, it's rather an internal operation that's designed to work in
tandem with operations that use private/public key pairs.
.Pp
Because the KEYMGMT operation shares knowledge with the operations it
works with in tandem, they must belong to the same provider.
The OpenSSL libraries will ensure that they do.
.Pp
The primary responsibility of the KEYMGMT operation is to hold the
provider side key data for the OpenSSL library EVP_PKEY structure.
.Pp
All "functions" mentioned here are passed as function pointers between
.Pa libcrypto
and the provider in
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
arrays via
.Xr OSSL_ALGORITHM 3
arrays that are returned by the provider's
.Xr provider_query_operation 3
function (see
.Xr provider-base 7 Ns ).
.Pp
All these "functions" have a corresponding function type definition
named
.Sy OSSL_FUNC_{name}_fn ,
and a helper function to retrieve the function pointer from a
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
element named
.Sy OSSL_FUNC_{name} .
For example, the "function"
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_keymgmt_new
has these:
.Bd -literal
 typedef void *(OSSL_FUNC_keymgmt_new_fn)(void *provctx);
 static ossl_inline OSSL_FUNC_keymgmt_new_fn
     OSSL_FUNC_keymgmt_new(const OSSL_DISPATCH *opf);
.Ed
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
arrays are indexed by numbers that are provided as macros in
.Xr openssl-core_dispatch.h 7 ,
as follows:
.Bd -literal
